# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — the NO-COMMA cell of the comma axis, a NEGATIVE.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is a deliberately
# UNFORMATTED INPUT — the assertion is on what `gg fmt` turns it INTO
# (`fmt_author_rows_no_comma_still_packs`, exact text). A sweep would rewrite it
# into its own expected output and the cell would go green-vacuous.
#
# AXIS = comma present/absent. Both containers below are hand-grouped into rows
# and NEITHER carries a magic trailing comma, so neither is exploded at all —
# the formatter PACKS them, which discards the rows. That is correct and
# ratified: author line grouping is not an independent explode signal, it only
# says WHERE to break inside a container something else already exploded.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE CELL THAT KEEPS THE FEATURE HONEST. If preservation ever became
# its own trigger, every packed multi-line list in the corpus would freeze into
# whatever shape it was last saved in, and `gg fmt` would stop being a
# normaliser. Two kinds are sampled — array literal and constructor call args —
# because the trigger lives at the shared chokepoint and a per-kind regression
# would show in only one.
#
# NO PRE-FIX RED IS POSSIBLE (this is preserved behaviour). RED-verified by
# BREAKING the mechanism: making the author-break probe an independent explode
# trigger — routing to `format_bracketed_broken_with_comments` on
# `magic || gated || engaged` — leaves both containers exploded into their
# author rows and the exact-text assertion reds. Restored after measuring.
from std.collections import Vector

struct Point:
    int x
    int y
    int z

void main():
    int y = 6
    Vector[int] xs = [
        1, 2,
        3, 4,
        5, y
    ]
    Point p = Point(
        1, 2,
        y
    )
    print(xs.len() + p.x + p.z)
